INT. GREENHOUSE, BOTANICAL GARDEN - EARLY MORNING

Glass and iron. Fog on the panes. Banana palms, orchids dripping with condensation, a stone bench in a clearing of ferns.

IZABELA FONTANAROSA (40s, garden curator, gardening gloves tucked in her belt, silver in her braid) sits on the bench with a thermos.

ARSLAN DEMIRTAŞ-WELLS (40s, visiting botanist, rain-damp coat, field notebook) stands in the doorway, dripping.

ARSLAN
Is this the Victorian wing?

IZABELA
It's the only wing.

ARSLAN
Then I'm in the right place.

IZABELA
You're wet.

ARSLAN
I walked from the station.

IZABELA
It's eleven miles.

ARSLAN
I like to walk.

IZABELA
You like to make a point.

ARSLAN
I like to make a point while walking.

She pours a second cup from the thermos and sets it on the bench beside her. He crosses the clearing and sits. Takes the cup. Drinks. Closes his eyes.

ARSLAN (CONT'D)
That's not coffee.

IZABELA
Chicory. I stopped drinking coffee the year you left.

ARSLAN
You hated coffee before I left.

IZABELA
I hated how you made it.

ARSLAN
I made it with love.

IZABELA
You made it with a blowtorch.

ARSLAN
The stove was broken.

IZABELA
The stove was fine. You liked the blowtorch.

ARSLAN
It had personality.

IZABELA
So did the smoke alarm.

ARSLAN
You kept that.

IZABELA
I replace the battery every spring.

ARSLAN
Why?

IZABELA
Because you'd have wanted me to.

He puts the cup down. Turns on the bench to face her.

ARSLAN
Izabela, I got your letter about the orchid.

IZABELA
The Paphiopedilum.

ARSLAN
The Paphiopedilum.

IZABELA
It bloomed.

ARSLAN
After eleven years?

IZABELA
Eleven years, two months, nine days.

ARSLAN
You counted.

IZABELA
I'm a curator. I count things.

ARSLAN
I'd like to see it.

IZABELA
It's behind you.

He turns. Between two ferns, in a shaft of gray morning light, a slipper orchid hangs, mottled maroon and green. He stands. Walks to it. Touches nothing.

ARSLAN
Oh.

IZABELA
Yes.

ARSLAN
It looks like you.

IZABELA
Don't be absurd.

ARSLAN
Stubborn, and magnificent, and not at all pleased to see me.

IZABELA
I'm pleased.

ARSLAN
You're not smiling.

IZABELA
I'm smiling with my gloves on.

ARSLAN
Take them off.

IZABELA
They're in my belt.

ARSLAN
Then take them out.

She laughs, and it surprises her. She stands and joins him at the orchid. They look at it together for a long time.

ARSLAN (CONT'D)
Eleven years.

IZABELA
Two months, nine days.

ARSLAN
I'd like to stay for the second bloom.

IZABELA
That could take another eleven years.

ARSLAN
I walked eleven miles.

IZABELA
You did.

ARSLAN
I can wait.
